DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These macros define and operate on singly linked tail queues.

       In the macro definitions, _T_Y_P_E is the name of a user‐defined structure,
       that  must contain a field of type _S_T_A_I_L_Q___E_N_T_R_Y, named _N_A_M_E.  The argu‐
       ment _H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E is the name of a user‐defined structure that must be  de‐
       clared using the macro SSTTAAIILLQQ__HHEEAADD().

   CCrreeaattiioonn
       A  singly  linked  tail  queue  is headed by a structure defined by the
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__HHEEAADD() macro.  This structure contains a pair of  pointers,  one
       to  the  first element in the tail queue and the other to the last ele‐
       ment in the tail queue.  The elements are  singly  linked  for  minimum
       space  and pointer manipulation overhead at the expense of O(n) removal
       for arbitrary elements.  New elements can be added to  the  tail  queue
       after an existing element, at the head of the tail queue, or at the end
       of the tail queue.  A _S_T_A_I_L_Q___H_E_A_D structure is declared as follows:

           STAILQ_HEAD(HEADNAME, TYPE) head;

       where  _s_t_r_u_c_t  _H_E_A_D_N_A_M_E is the structure to be defined, and _s_t_r_u_c_t _T_Y_P_E
       is the type of the elements to  be  linked  into  the  tail  queue.   A
       pointer to the head of the tail queue can later be declared as:

           struct HEADNAME *headp;

       (The names _h_e_a_d and _h_e_a_d_p are user selectable.)

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__EENNTTRRYY()  declares  a structure that connects the elements in the
       tail queue.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__HHEEAADD__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() evaluates to  an  initializer  for  the  tail
       queue _h_e_a_d.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__IINNIITT() initializes the tail queue referenced by _h_e_a_d.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__EEMMPPTTYY()  evaluates  to  true  if  there are no items on the tail
       queue.

   IInnsseerrttiioonn
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__IINNSSEERRTT__HHEEAADD() inserts the new element _e_l_m at  the  head  of  the
       tail queue.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__IINNSSEERRTT__TTAAIILL() inserts the new element _e_l_m at the end of the tail
       queue.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__IINNSSEERRTT__AAFFTTEERR()  inserts  the  new  element _e_l_m after the element
       _l_i_s_t_e_l_m.

   TTrraavveerrssaall
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__FFIIRRSSTT() returns the first item on the tail queue or NULL if  the
       tail queue is empty.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__NNEEXXTT()  returns  the  next  item on the tail queue, or NULL this
       item is the last.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__FFOORREEAACCHH() traverses the tail queue referenced  by  _h_e_a_d  in  the
       forward direction, assigning each element in turn to _v_a_r.

   RReemmoovvaall
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__RREEMMOOVVEE() removes the element _e_l_m from the tail queue.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__RREEMMOOVVEE__HHEEAADD() removes the element at the head of the tail queue.
       For  optimum  efficiency,  elements  being removed from the head of the
       tail queue should use this macro explicitly  rather  than  the  generic
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__RREEMMOOVVEE() macro.

   OOtthheerr ffeeaattuurreess
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__CCOONNCCAATT()  concatenates  the  tail queue headed by _h_e_a_d_2 onto the
       end of the one headed by _h_e_a_d_1 removing all entries from the former.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__EEMMPPTTYY() returns nonzero if the queue is empty, and zero  if  the
       queue contains at least one entry.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__FFIIRRSSTT(), and SSTTAAIILLQQ__NNEEXXTT() return a pointer to the first or next
       _T_Y_P_E structure, respectively.

       SSTTAAIILLQQ__HHEEAADD__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR()  returns  an initializer that can be assigned
       to the queue _h_e_a_d.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       Some BSDs provide SIMPLEQ instead of STAILQ.  They are  identical,  but
       for  historical  reasons they were named differently on different BSDs.
       STAILQ originated on FreeBSD, and SIMPLEQ originated  on  NetBSD.   For
       compatibility reasons, some systems provide both sets of macros.  glibc
       provides  both  STAILQ  and  SIMPLEQ,  which are identical except for a
       missing SIMPLEQ equivalent to SSTTAAIILLQQ__CCOONNCCAATT().

BBUUGGSS
       SSTTAAIILLQQ__FFOORREEAACCHH() doesn’t allow _v_a_r to be removed or  freed  within  the
       loop, as it would interfere with the traversal.  SSTTAAIILLQQ__FFOORREEAACCHH__SSAAFFEE(),
       which  is  present  on the BSDs but is not present in glibc, fixes this
       limitation by allowing _v_a_r to safely be removed from the list and freed
       from within the loop without interfering with the traversal.

